Accept xz stream padding that runs to the end of the input buffer.
After a stream ends, the decoder skips the zero bytes of stream padding
and resets itself when the next stream begins. When the padding ran to
the end of the bytes read so far, the loop fell through and called the
decoder anyway, still positioned past the old stream's footer. It made
no progress, so at end of input the decoder reported a buffer error and
a valid file ending in padding failed with "corrupted data", as did
concatenated streams whose padding crossed a read boundary. The patch
reads more input instead, so end of input after padding is a clean end
and a following stream is found where it starts.

---
 archival/libarchive/decompress_unxz.c |    2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

--- a/archival/libarchive/decompress_unxz.c
+++ b/archival/libarchive/decompress_unxz.c
@@ -119,6 +119,8 @@
 				}
 				iobuf.in_pos++;
 			} while (iobuf.in_pos < iobuf.in_size);
+			/* All of it was padding: EOF or more data may follow */
+			continue;
 		}
  do_run:
 //		bb_error_msg(">in pos:%d size:%d out pos:%d size:%d",
